      "text": "filename: docs/savings-basics.md\n# Understanding Savings Accounts: A Beginner's Guide\n\nA savings account is a type of bank account designed to help you save money securely while earning interest. It's a simple yet powerful tool for building financial stability. In this guide, we'll explore four key concepts: interest, deposit, withdraw, and balance.\n\n## What is Interest?\n\nInterest is the amount of money a bank pays you for keeping your money in a savings account. It's usually calculated as a percentage of your balance and added to your account regularly. The interest rate can vary depending on the bank and the type of savings account you choose. Over time, interest can help your savings grow, making it an important factor to consider when choosing a savings account.\n\n## How to Make a Deposit\n\nA deposit is when you add money to your savings account. This can be done through various methods, such as in-person at a bank branch, online via a mobile app, or through automatic transfers from your paycheck. Depositing money into your savings account is a straightforward process that helps you build your savings over time.\n\n## How to Withdraw Money\n\nA withdrawal is when you take money out of your savings account. This can be done in person, online, or through a mobile app. While withdrawals are allowed, it's important to note that some savings accounts may have limits on how much you can withdraw in a given period. Understanding these limits can help you manage your savings effectively.\n\n## What is a Balance?\n\nA balance is the total amount of money in your savings account at any given time. It includes all the money you've deposited minus any withdrawals you've made. Checking your balance regularly helps you keep track of your savings and ensures you're on track to meet your financial goals.\n\n## Why These Concepts Matter\n\nUnderstanding interest, deposits, withdrawals, and balances is essential for managing your savings account effectively. These concepts work together to help you grow your money, track your progress, and make informed financial decisions.\n\nBy familiarizing yourself with these basics, you'll be well on your way to building a strong foundation for your financial future.",
